WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:02.490
INTRO:
Following the dotcom crash, as money dried

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:07.560
up and the company’s stock crashed 95%,
the executives set up an emergency meeting.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:12.790
While their eCommerce business was still growing
rapidly, it was far from being profitable.

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:16.940
So, they decided to launch a platform that
helped other companies to build their own

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:19.760
eCommerce websites called Merchant.com.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.800
Hearing this story, you probably thought that
this is how Shopify got started right?

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.480
Well, this was actually the story of Amazon
and their diversification effort after the

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:30.509
dot-com crash.

00:00:30.509 --> 00:00:33.570
But, this wasn’t actually all that successful.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:39.220
You see, Amazon’s rapid growth throughout
the 1990s had led to a rather unorganized

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:44.070
business environment which made it extremely
difficult for them successfully launch merchant.com.

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:49.500
So, Amazon quickly dropped the idea started
testing out other paths for diversification.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:56.470
6 years later though, in 2006, Shopify, leveraged
the same business model as merchant.com, and

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.470
they knocked it out of the park.

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:05.020
So, here’s the story of Shopify and how
they showed up Amazon at their own game.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:10.080
TOBIAS LÜTKE:
Taking a look back, the story of Shopify circles

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.650
back to a man named Tobias Lutke who was born
in 1981 in Koblenz, West Germany.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:19.660
His father was an internal medicine doctor
while his mother was a teacher for disabled

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:20.660
children.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:24.820
From a very young age, Tobi had a massive
interest in modifying computer hardware and

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:25.820
coding.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:30.780
So, when he was 6 years old, his parents bought
him a computer similar to the Commodore 64.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:34.830
And within the next few years, Tobi taught
himself how to write code and develop simple

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:36.040
games.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:40.030
Despite this innate curiosity to experiment
and learn by himself, it doesn’t seem like

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:42.470
Tobi was a big fan of school.

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:46.670
Whenever a teacher asked him a question, Tobi
preferred to give them smart witty responses

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:49.130
instead of actually answering the question.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:53.220
And he took shortcuts whenever possible and
tried to pass his classes by spending the

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.300
least amount of effort possible.

00:01:55.300 --> 00:02:00.020
In my opinion, I think Tobi simply had his
priorities straight from a very young age,

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.150
but from his parents’ and teachers’ perspectives,
this was quite concerning.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:09.510
So, they had Tobi take a bunch of psychoanalysis
exams to identify if he had any learning disabilities,

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:12.180
but they were never able to diagnose anything.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.459
And as Tobi grew older, his behavior from
a traditional perspective simply got worse

00:02:16.459 --> 00:02:17.560
and worse.

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:22.290
Tobi says that he had a serious authority
problem and hated answering to teachers.

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:27.400
So, as soon as he could drop out of school,
he did at age 16 from 10th grade.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:32.200
Tobi went ahead and joined a computer science
apprentice program at Siemens, but he didn’t

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:33.410
like this very much either.

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:38.340
You see, the apprenticeship required him to
use Java which he felt was a super restrictive

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:39.730
programming language.

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:44.800
In this world of restrictions and limitations,
Tobi felt that there was only one true escape

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.290
which was snowboarding.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:50.349
Tobi would regularly go on snowboarding trips
and one of these trips took him to Whistler,

00:02:50.349 --> 00:02:52.970
British Columbia where he met a girl named
Fiona Mckean.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:57.150
As you would guess, these two would fall in
love, and due to the great distance between

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:01.300
Germany and Canada, they would have to figure
out a living situation.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:04.820
Initially, they agreed to live in each other’s
countries in alternating years.

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:10.130
First, Fiona came to Germany and lived with
Tobi for a year, and then Tobi moved to Canada

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:14.090
to live with Fiona for a year, but he never
came.

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:19.050
SNOWBOARDING BUSINESS:
Despite finding the love of his life, Tobi

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:20.720
was still kind of a bum.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:24.599
At the end of the day, he was still a high
school dropout that had no idea what he wanted

00:03:24.599 --> 00:03:28.560
to do, so he started networking with people
in Canada.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.840
One of the first people he ran into was a
man named Scott Lake.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:36.420
Scott was a family friend of Fiona and would
regularly come to family events, and this

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:37.959
is when he would meet Tobi.

00:03:37.959 --> 00:03:42.351
Tobi was significantly younger than Scott
coming in at 12 years younger, but the two

00:03:42.351 --> 00:03:46.330
had several similar interests, and soon enough,
they decided to start a business together

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:47.830
in 2004.

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:51.170
The initial plan wasn’t anything crazy or
revolutionary.

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:54.740
The duo just wanted to sell something that
they were both passionate about, snowboards,

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:55.880
through the internet.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:59.060
Though the idea was simple, they wanted it
to be personal.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:02.800
In other words, they didn’t want to just
flip snowboards on eBay.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:08.000
So, they decided to launch their own eCommerce
website, but all the eCommerce software available

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.160
at the time was absolute crap according to
Tobi.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.670
So, Tobi decided to develop his own.

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:18.239
As you might expect with Tobi though, he refused
to use any of the popular programming languages

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:20.910
because he thought all of them were too restricting.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:25.569
So, instead, he used an obscure Japanese programming
language called Ruby.

00:04:25.569 --> 00:04:29.879
Ruby was so obscure that it didn’t even
have written documentation in English.

00:04:29.879 --> 00:04:34.539
Luckily for Tobi though, a Danish programmer
named David Hansson would develop a new framework

00:04:34.539 --> 00:04:38.889
for Ruby around the same time which made it
much easier for Tobi to code.

00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:42.289
This new framework also generated a lot of
interest within the coding space regarding

00:04:42.289 --> 00:04:46.689
Ruby which got Tobi involved in several online
forums relating to Ruby.

00:04:46.689 --> 00:04:50.409
Tobi shared a lot of his software on these
forums such as his software for processing

00:04:50.409 --> 00:04:53.960
credit cards, and he quickly became a leading
voice within the community.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:58.360
In the meantime, Tobi and Scott were also
packaging up snowboards and sending them to

00:04:58.360 --> 00:04:59.360
customers.

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:03.419
But, given all the interest online surrounding
Tobi’s software, it became pretty clear

00:05:03.419 --> 00:05:08.099
to Tobi and Scott that selling software was
the way to go.

00:05:08.099 --> 00:05:12.389
SHOPIFY:
Despite this grand vision, neither Tobi nor

00:05:12.389 --> 00:05:16.729
Scott had much money, so they started raising
money from their friends and family.

00:05:16.729 --> 00:05:21.449
They were able to raise a total of $200,000
from 10 people the biggest investors being

00:05:21.449 --> 00:05:24.190
Fiona’s father Bruce McKean and Tobi’s
uncle.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:28.400
Fun fact, this investment would end up making
Bruce a billionaire.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:32.999
Anyway, using these funds, our duo went ahead
and hired a German programmer named Daniel

00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:36.759
Weinand who they consider to be a cofounder
of Shopify.

00:05:36.759 --> 00:05:41.100
The name Shopify has actually been around
since the start of the company, but the company

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.509
wasn’t actually originally called Shopify.

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:49.430
The company was actually called Jaded Pixel
and Shopify was just the name of the software.

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:53.569
Given the clout Tobi had amongst online forums,
they were quickly able to build up a base

00:05:53.569 --> 00:05:57.930
use of customers and start pulling in monthly
revenue of about $8000.

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:03.120
Over the next two years, this number would
grow almost 10 fold to $60,000 per month,

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:07.419
but this also forced Tobi to raise a lot more
money which he didn’t really want to do.

00:06:07.419 --> 00:06:11.889
It’s not that Tobi didn’t want to bring
on investors, but rather that he didn’t

00:06:11.889 --> 00:06:15.139
want to bring on venture capitalists who he
thought would ruin the company.

00:06:15.139 --> 00:06:19.039
Scott strongly disagreed with this perspective
and felt that venture capital was the way

00:06:19.039 --> 00:06:20.039
to go.

00:06:20.039 --> 00:06:23.899
And given the company’s desperate need for
cash, Tobi gave in and started pitching to

00:06:23.899 --> 00:06:28.699
VCs, but no VC really wanted to invest in
an eCommerce business.

00:06:28.699 --> 00:06:32.529
They felt like they had already tried that
in the 1990s which resulted in the dot-com

00:06:32.529 --> 00:06:36.849
crash and massive losses, so why would they
try that again.

00:06:36.849 --> 00:06:40.680
After failing to raise more capital, Scott
would leave the company, and Tobi would become

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:42.619
the CEO of Shopify.

00:06:42.619 --> 00:06:47.720
Tobi didn’t really want to be CEO, he just
wanted to code great software, but with the

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:51.689
future of the company at stake, Tobi would
try to teach himself business.

00:06:51.689 --> 00:06:56.289
He started meeting with a lot more VCs, but
these meetings just exposed how clueless he

00:06:56.289 --> 00:06:57.289
was.

00:06:57.289 --> 00:07:02.059
Tobi wasn’t familiar with technical business
terminology like margin ratios, so he struggled

00:07:02.059 --> 00:07:04.020
to answer these questions when VCs asked him.

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:06.349
But, Tobi didn’t let this stop him.

00:07:06.349 --> 00:07:10.429
After every VC meeting, Tobi would learn about
the terms that he didn’t know which allowed

00:07:10.429 --> 00:07:13.419
him to approach the next meeting with just
a bit more knowledge.

00:07:13.419 --> 00:07:19.180
Slowly, Tobi’s VC meetings started to get
better and better, but then the 2008 financial

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:23.479
recession hit which basically made it impossible
for an eCommerce company to raise money.

00:07:23.479 --> 00:07:29.169
And with that, Tobi knew that he was truly
on his own.

00:07:29.169 --> 00:07:32.490
REFORMATION:
The next two years would end up being the

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:34.770
roughest years of Shopify’s history.

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:39.000
Tobi and Fiona had to live with Fiona’s
parents, and her father had to fund payroll

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:40.349
from savings.

00:07:40.349 --> 00:07:43.919
Tobi says that the situation was extremely
demoralizing.

00:07:43.919 --> 00:07:48.539
Every day at meetings, Tobi discussed the
long-term goals and vision of Shopify, but

00:07:48.539 --> 00:07:53.349
the truth was that it wasn’t clear if Shopify
would even survive the next few months.

00:07:53.349 --> 00:07:55.809
Tobi refused to focus on the short term though.

00:07:55.809 --> 00:08:00.439
He was adamant that they needed to think long-term
for the company to truly be successful.

00:08:00.439 --> 00:08:03.589
And since he couldn’t do too much about
the company’s financial situation for the

00:08:03.589 --> 00:08:07.369
time being, he decided to focus on building
a phenomenal team.

00:08:07.369 --> 00:08:10.740
You would think that Tobi was looking for
genius programmers that were willing to go

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:15.009
all-in on Shopify, but this couldn’t be
further from the truth.

00:08:15.009 --> 00:08:19.539
Tobi says that he would hire a team player
over a brilliant jerk any day of the week.

00:08:19.539 --> 00:08:23.639
Also, despite all this hardship, Tobi never
pulled an Elon.

00:08:23.639 --> 00:08:28.599
He refused to work 100 hour weeks and he refused
to force employees to do that either.

00:08:28.599 --> 00:08:32.279
Tobi strongly believes with creative work,
you can’t cheat.

00:08:32.279 --> 00:08:36.460
He says that everyone only has 5 creative
hours per day, and trying to work for longer

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:38.950
hours doesn’t lead to better results.

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:43.000
While this strongly goes against all the beliefs
of Silicon Valley, Tobi stuck with it even

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:44.640
during the worst of times.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:48.630
And as the world slowly recovered from the
financial recession, the interest in eCommerce

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:52.580
started to come back to life, and Shopify
was finally able to achieve profitability

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:54.460
by 2010.

00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:59.370
Around the same time, VCs became more open
to investing in eCommerce once again and Bessemer

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:04.030
Venture Partners would agree to invest $7
million into Shopify for 20% of the company

00:09:04.030 --> 00:09:06.050
in December of 2010.

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:11.110
The following year, Shopify would raise another
$15 million and in 2013, they would raise

00:09:11.110 --> 00:09:12.110
$100 million.

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:16.830
Tobi would top all of this off by taking Shopify
public in 2015 which valued the company at

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:18.870
just over $1 billion.

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:22.990
Tobi had successfully navigated the company
through the recession and built a team that

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:29.410
was stronger than ever before, but Shopify
was yet to even receive their biggest push.

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:33.490
DROPSHIPPING:
Up until this point, Shopify mainly appealed

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:37.000
to small business owners who wanted to expand
their business through the internet.

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:42.130
But in 2017 and 2018, we saw the rise of one
of the most infamous get-rich-quick schemes

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:43.510
of all time: dropshipping.

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:48.020
If you’re not familiar with dropshipping,
it’s basically the idea to start an online

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:52.580
store using Shopify, sourcing products on
Alibaba or Aliexpress, and then linking the

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:53.690
two together.

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:58.190
This way, you don’t have to physically deal
with the product and your job is to just market

00:09:58.190 --> 00:09:59.190
it.

00:09:59.190 --> 00:10:02.050
While it sounds simple in theory, it’s actually
extremely difficult.

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:06.350
Now, this isn’t to say that you can’t
make great money through dropshipping, but

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:10.500
like with any business, the vast majority
fail and it’s not nearly as easy as the

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:12.640
dropshipping gurus make it out to be.

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:16.780
Nonetheless, this explosive trend towards
dropshipping was extremely lucrative for Shopify

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:19.500
and sent them to the moon.

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:23.510
Shopify reached a peak market cap of $212
billion.

00:10:23.510 --> 00:10:27.770
Since then, Shopify stock has actually gotten
destroyed along with the rest of the market

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:29.170
dropping over 60%.

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:34.900
This has crumbled Shopify’s market cap down
to just over $80 billion, but this is really

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:36.430
nothing to worry about.

00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:40.350
If we look at Shopify’s revenue and gross
profit graphs, they haven’t even had a single

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:41.350
negative quarter.

00:10:41.350 --> 00:10:45.510
So, I find it hard to believe that Shopify
will be fundamentally struggling any time

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:46.510
soon.

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:51.240
As for Tobi, his net worth peaked at over
$10 billion, but it has since cooled down

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:52.740
to just under $6 billion.

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:55.070
Tobi doesn’t really care that much about
these numbers though.

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:59.090
While he’s super passionate about Shopify
and grateful that he’s able to work on what

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:04.030
he loves, he also believes that at the end
of the day, Shopify is just a job.

00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:08.220
And no job will ever take priority over his
family or personal health.

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:12.190
Just like at the beginning, it looks like
Tobi still has his priorities straight.

00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:14.760
What do you guys think about Tobi’s approach
to business?

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:18.970
Do you agree that humans shouldn’t work
past a certain point regardless of the situation?

00:11:18.970 --> 00:11:19.970
Comment that down below.

00:11:19.970 --> 00:11:24.850
Also, drop a like if you appreciate Tobi being
the number one donator to Team Trees.

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:28.660
And of course, consider checking out our international
channels to watch our videos in other languages

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:41.450
and consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

